    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Major depressive disorder, recurrent, severe (F33.2)</content>. Partial response at six weeks: PHQ-9 15 from 21, a 29% fall, short of the 50% that defines response. Tolerating sertraline. Safety reviewed: no thoughts of suicide or self-harm. [S]<list><item>Increase sertraline to 100 mg daily.</item><item>Continue weekly psychotherapy.</item><item>Review in five weeks.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Tourette syndrome (F95.2)</content>. Behavioral therapy for tics under way. [S]<list><item>Continue the eight sessions.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression measure (PHQ-9)</td><td>At baseline and at least monthly during acute treatment; every 2 to 4 weeks in continuation</td><td>Completed today</td><td>15, from 21: partial response</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Sertraline adherence and adverse effects</td><td>Every visit in the acute phase; reassess at 2 weeks after starting and after each dose change</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No side effects; increased to 100 mg</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide-risk review (PHQ-9 item 9 and structured questions since the last visit), safety plan, and the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No thoughts of suicide or self-harm</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Psychotherapy (cognitive behavioral therapy with behavioral activation)</td><td>Offer to all with moderate or severe depression</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Five sessions</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Anxiety measure (GAD-7)</td><td>At baseline and with PHQ-9 reviews</td><td>Completed today</td><td>9, from 12</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Tic severity (Yale Global Tic Severity Scale, recorded in the note; no LOINC code is used)</td><td>At diagnosis and periodically to judge the need for and response to treatment</td><td>Due</td><td>At three months</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Comprehensive Behavioral Intervention for Tics</td><td>Offer as an initial treatment option</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Two of eight sessions</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Tic-suppressing medication (alpha-2 agonist, aripiprazole, or other)</td><td>When tics cause impairment that behavioral therapy does not relieve</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Not needed</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>PHQ-9 response (at least a 50% fall) by three months and remission (below 5) by six months. In progress; target 1765-09-28 [S]</item><item>Back at daily writing and seeing friends again by three months. In progress; target 1765-06-22 [S]</item><item>Fewer and less disruptive tics, judged by him and by the tic severity scale, by six months. In progress; target 1765-09-28 [S]</item><item>Safety plan in place and reviewed at every visit. In progress; target 1765-09-28 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Practice guideline recommendations summary: Treatment of tics in people with Tourette syndrome and chronic tic disorders (Pringsheim T, et al. Neurology 2019;92(19):896-906) (2019); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Major Depressive Disorder (2022); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Review in five weeks. Psychotherapy weekly; tic therapy weekly. Next scheduled visit 1765-06-22: eleven-week review of depression and tics. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
